# boardid.config
#
# erlinit runs this (-d /usr/bin/boardid) to build the hostname, so it is
# what keeps two Fairphone 3s apart. That matters for more than
# cosmetics: VintageNetDirect's DHCP server picks the usb0 /30 by hashing
# the hostname into 172.31.0.0/16, so two phones answering to the same
# name also claim the same address — measured, with both plugged into one
# laptop and each link holding 172.31.242.54/30.
#
# The original version of this file read `-n 8` and ended in `|| true`.
# `-n` sets how many characters to print, not how many bytes to read
# (that is `-l`), and `|| true` is shell syntax in a file that is not
# shell. Every FP3 fell through to cpuinfo, which carries no serial on
# arm64, and came up as "0".

# The Qualcomm SoC serial, exposed by the mainline soc driver. The best
# identifier on this board — but it arrives via SMEM, whose probe can be
# deferred, and erlinit reads the ID early: with only this line the
# hostname still came up as nerves-0000 while `boardid` on the running
# phone answered 3669959182 quite happily.
#
# `-t text` emits the digits as they appear in sysfs; without it the
# bytes are hex-encoded and 3669959182 prints as 33363639393539313832.
-b binfile -f /sys/devices/soc0/serial_number -l 10 -t text

# The eMMC CID, for when the above is not populated yet. This cannot be
# late: the rootfs was just mounted from this device.
#
# Offset 20 skips the manufacturer and product-name fields, which are
# identical on both phones here (15010052…), and lands on the card's
# serial number — fd3197f8 versus fa67d281 on the two in use. Reading
# from 0 would give every FP3 the same first four characters, and the
# hostname pattern only uses four.
-b binfile -f /sys/block/mmcblk0/device/cid -k 20 -l 8 -t text

# Last resort, so a device without either still gets an ID.
-b cpuinfo
